{"name":"Aquarium Blue","hex":"#66cdaa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aquarium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tank, often made of glass, for keeping live fish or other aquatic animals.","A public place where live fish and other aquatic animals are exhibited."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aquarium"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aquarium","blocks":[{"text":"An aquarium is a vivarium of any size having at least one transparent side in which aquatic plants or animals are kept and displayed. Fishkeepers use aquaria to keep fish, invertebrates, amphibians, aquatic reptiles, such as turtles, and aquatic plants. The term aquarium, coined by English naturalist Philip Henry Gosse, combines the Latin root aqua, meaning \"water,\" with the suffix -arium, meaning \"a place for relating to or connected with.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Amaterske_akvarium.jpg/330px-Amaterske_akvarium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquarium","wikidata":"Q45782"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg/500px-Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg/1280px-Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg/500px-Underwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The underwater tunnel in the Sea Life London aquarium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUnderwater_Walk_of_Sea_Life_London_Aquarium.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Cat_by_Koryusai.jpg/500px-Cat_by_Koryusai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Cat_by_Koryusai.jpg/500px-Cat_by_Koryusai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cat and fishbowl, by Isoda Koryusai. Original c. 1775.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACat_by_Koryusai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Waldm%C3%BCller_-_Die_Schauspielerin_Theres_Krones.jpe